    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Full sit-ups load the low back more than curl-ups: sit-ups gave higher compression than partial curl-ups in McGill's data (McGill 1998; Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion work.",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated loaded spinal flexion and twisting.",
        "The athlete mesh rests on the mat; the rig collision shapes are thicker than the mesh at the back and buttocks, so floorTolerance records how far those proxy shapes sink below the mat surface."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Neck pain: cross the arms on the chest.",
        "Low-back discomfort: stop at a three-quarter sit-up or a crunch.",
        "Unanchored feet shift the work toward the abdominals (smaller range)."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Crunch.",
        "Three-quarter sit-up.",
        "Sit-up with the hands reaching to the knees."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Arms crossed on the chest, then fingertips behind the head.",
        "Slow 3 s lowering.",
        "Hold a light plate at arm's length."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis curls the trunk off the mat and holds the pelvis as the hips flex: about 40% (upper) and 35% (lower) MVIC in a bent-knee sit-up (Escamilla 2016). In Konrad 2001 the sit-up kept rectus activity at the curl-up level while external oblique rose to about 57% MVC. Reaching the arms forward shifts the load toward the hips, so this is the easiest arm position for a sit-up (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Iliopsoas and rectus femoris take over once the shoulder blades are off the mat and pull the trunk up to the thighs. Flexed and supported (anchored) legs increased hip-flexor activation in hip-flexion sit-ups without changing abdominal activity (Andersson 1997).",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Iliopsoas and rectus femoris take over once the shoulder blades are off the mat and pull the trunk up to the thighs. Flexed and supported (anchored) legs increased hip-flexor activation in hip-flexion sit-ups without changing abdominal activity (Andersson 1997).",
      "obliques_r": "External obliques were about 36% MVIC in a bent-knee sit-up (Escamilla 2016) and about 57% MVC in Konrad 2001; they steady the trunk on the way up and down.",
      "obliques_l": "External obliques were about 36% MVIC in a bent-knee sit-up (Escamilla 2016) and about 57% MVC in Konrad 2001; they steady the trunk on the way up and down.",
      "quads_r": "Rectus femoris works as a hip flexor: about 23% MVIC in a bent-knee sit-up versus 6% in a crunch (Escamilla 2016), and it was higher in the traditional (hip-flexion) sit-up than in a trunk-flexion version (J Strength Cond Res 2015).",
      "quads_l": "Rectus femoris works as a hip flexor: about 23% MVIC in a bent-knee sit-up versus 6% in a crunch (Escamilla 2016), and it was higher in the traditional (hip-flexion) sit-up than in a trunk-flexion version (J Strength Cond Res 2015).",
      "adductors_r": "Keep the knees in line over the anchored feet (clinical opinion).",
      "adductors_l": "Keep the knees in line over the anchored feet (clinical opinion)."
    }
